Day 4 – Enable Versioning on an Amazon S3 Bucket (AWS)

Objective

Enable versioning on the S3 bucket nautilus-s3-618805932 so that accidental deletions and overwrites can be recovered.

Configuration
Setting                  Value
Bucket Name              nautilus-s3-618805932
AWS Region               US East (N. Virginia) us-east-1
Bucket Versioning        Enabled
MFA Delete               Disabled

Steps Performed
1. Opened the AWS S3 Console.
2. Selected the bucket nautilus-s3-618805932.
3. Opened the Properties tab.
4. Under Bucket Versioning, clicked Edit.
5. Enabled bucket versioning and saved.
6. Confirmed the success banner "Successfully edited Bucket Versioning" and verified the status shows Enabled.

Important Concepts Learned
- S3 Versioning: Keeps every version of an object in the same bucket, so older data can be restored after an overwrite or deletion.
- Delete marker: Deleting an object without a version ID only adds a delete marker. Removing that marker restores the previous current version.
- Version ID: Each version has a unique ID that can be used to restore or copy a known-good version.
- Permanent delete: Deleting a specific version ID removes that version for good, so versioning should be combined with IAM permissions and lifecycle rules.
- MFA Delete: An extra safeguard that requires multi-factor authentication to change versioning or permanently delete versions. It is set only through the AWS CLI, SDK or REST API.

Key Takeaway
Versioning protects S3 data from accidental deletion and corruption. Enable it on buckets that hold important data, and control who can permanently delete versions.
